>> title: Chinese Five-Spice Powder
>> category: Spice Blends & Marinades
>> tags: chinese, spice blend, toasted, pantry
>> counters: Dim Sum Counter, Takeout Counter
>> aka: five spice, ng heung fun, wu xiang fen, 五香粉
>> pairs-with: red-braised-pork-belly
>> servings: 16
>> time: 15 min
>> slack: narrow — the cloves go in off the heat, because clove burns first and burnt clove is all you can taste afterwards

>> step: toast in a dry skillet 3 min
Toast @star anise{3%whole}(broken into points), @Sichuan peppercorns{2%tsp}, @fennel seeds{2%tsp}, and @cloves{1%tsp} in a dry #skillet{} over medium-low heat until fragrant, about ~{3%min}.

>> step: add off the heat
Add @cassia bark{1}(3-in piece, broken up; or cinnamon stick) to @&(~1)toasted spices{} off the heat.

>> step: cool, grind, sift fine
Cool @&(~1)spices{} completely, grind to a powder in a #spice grinder{}, then sift through a fine #sieve{}.
